e dgs patient you would see  ventricular septal defect  or a true ventricular canal defect these  kind of defects are pretty common  in d george syndrome patients other than  heart there would be complications  associated with thyroid and parathyroid  gland  now let me tell you another important  feature of d george syndrome which is  present  of a cleft pellet so here  a opening or cleft in the roof of the  mouth plate  is seen so you can see the  the upper lips are not totally joined to  each other  now as a result of this there could be  difficulties in swallowing  and there could be also speech related  or verbal  difficulties characteristic sound might  come out  so this kind of difficult this kind of  differences are present in dijon  syndrome  questions now although most of the  uh dijord syndrome patients suffer to  some extent immunodeficiency  but the the extent of immunodeficiency  could be  variable now the most important thing  is thymus  is not present or partially present in  these george syndrome patients  so you can clearly understand t cells  which develop from the thymus  is not present in case of these dijon  syndrome or even if presents at a very  small  extent so all the t cell mediated  functions of the immune systems are  abrogated  and these patients are more vulnerable  towards opportunistic pathogen bacteria  etc  now thymic transplantation and passive  antibody treatment  might be beneficial for these patients  but this  is not a long-term solution so these  individuals  have their cell mediated immunity  severely compromised  as well as the b cell mediated uh  humoral immunity is also compromised  because b cell activation requires t  cell so that is why these individuals  are vulnerable to bacterial and fungal  infection  so i hope that summarize a little bit  about d george syndrome and this is a  very short video  but if you like this video give it a  quick thumbs up don't forget to like  share and subscribe  thank you and if you want